A polyester film featuring internal cavities is described here. This film comprises a central layer (referred to as layer A) that contains cavities within it, along with additional layers (designated as layers B) that are applied to both faces of layer A. Each of these B layers is made from a polyester resin that incorporates inorganic particles.

Layer A is formulated from a composition that combines a polyester resin together with a polypropylene resin. This polypropylene resin must meet the following criteria:

(1) Its melt flow rate (MFR) falls between 1.0 and 10 g per 10 minutes, as measured at 230°C with an applied load of 2.16 kg;

(2) Under a bending stress of 0.45 MPa, its deflection temperature reaches at least 85°C;

(3) When analyzed using gel permeation chromatography (GPC), its weight-average molecular weight Mw is between 200,000 and 450,000. Additionally, the distribution of molecular weight—expressed as the quotient of the weight-average molecular weight Mw divided by the number-average molecular weight Mn (Mw/Mn)—lies within the range of 2 to 6.
